{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-149459","model_call":44,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"race/ethnicity\" \"gender\" \"disability\" \"religion\" \"sexual orientation\" bullying)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"race/ethnicity\" \"gender\" \"disability\" \"religion\" \"sexual orientation\" bullying)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_687138123#0_1518615831","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2291","rank":1,"snippet":"religious beliefs, or disability. Illegal acts that can be elevated to hate crimes include physical assault, bullying, harassment, insults, damage to property, graffiti, and other crimes committed in the name of prejudice. To explore this concept, consider the following hate crime definition. Definition of Hate Crime Noun A crime of violence that is motivated by prejudice or intolerance against an individual or group of people. Origin Civil Rights Act of 1871 What is a Hate Crime A hate crime is the intentional hurting or intimidating of someone because of his perceived characteristics that place him in a particular social group. The characteristics of protected groups of people include: <cover>Race Ethnicity Nationality Religion Gender and Gender Identity Sexual Orientation Disability Perpetrators of hate crimes are motivated by their personal bias against an individual or group of people belonging to a protected group. Perpetrators of this type of crime use methods ranging from bullying </cover>to physical violence and verbal threats to create fear in their victims.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Hate Crime - Definition, Examples, Cases\" , \"url\": \"https://legaldictionary.net/hate-crime/\" }"},"time":1022},"model_calls":44,"document_judgments":15,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
