{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-149459","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2292"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2292"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_251223477#3_650545664","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2292","rank":2,"snippet":"Victimization and Asian-American Students Myths About Asian-American Bullying and Victimization What Do We Know About Bullying and Victimization Among Asian-American Students? National Data Notable Findings From Local Studies: What Is Bullying? Statistics and Points to Keep in Mind References \" , \"segment\": \"Fewer Asian-American students (18 percent) reported being bullied at school or cyberbullied than did white students (35 percent), African-American students (31 percent) or Latino students (28 percent). More Asian-American victims of bullying (11.1 percent) said that they were <cover>bullied because of their race than did white victims (2.8 percent), African-American victims (7.1 percent) </cover>or Latino victims (6.2 percent). Like other racial minorities, more Asian-American students (11 percent) reported being frequently targeted with race-related hate words than was reported by White students (3 percent). Racial/ethnic minorities who break stereotypes are more likely to be bullied. Asian American and Latino student athletes were more likely to be bullied, whereas sport participation was an insulating factor for White and Black students. 5 Among Asian American students, immigrant and 2nd generation students were more likely to be victimized than 3rd or later generation students. ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_251223477#3_650545664","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":16,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
