{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-149459","model_call":72,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2312"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2312"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_94142915#1_185117954","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2312","rank":3,"snippet":"over the past year. The discrimination they detail primarily takes the form of verbal harassment, shunning and cyberbullying. The report was released Thursday by the Stop AAPI Hate Youth Campaign, a high school internship program at <cover>Stop AAPI Hate, a national center that collects reports of coronavirus discrimination against Asian Americans and Pacific Islanders. The study details the emotional toll of growing anti-Asian sentiment around the country. Nearly 8 of 10 respondents expressed anger over the epidemic of hate against Asians. The findings come from nearly 1,000 interviews of Asian American young adults conducted by a team of 87 Asian American high school students this summer. Full coverage of the coronavirus outbreak \"I think we hear this a lot, but the youth are the future,\" said Cassie Eng, a senior at the Urban School of San Francisco, who worked on the study. \"\" , \"start_char\": 826.000000 , \"title\": \"'You have Chinese virus!': 1 in 4 Asian American youths experience racist bullying, report says\" , \"url\": \"https://www.nbcnews.com/news/asian-america/25-percent-</cover>asian-american-youths-racist-bullying-n1240380\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_94142915#1_185117954","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":72,"document_judgments":26,"relevance_3":11,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-149459","model_call":72,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2312"},"model_calls":72,"document_judgments":26,"relevance_3":11,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-149459","model_call":72,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2312"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2312"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_94142915#0_185115709","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2312","rank":4,"snippet":"<cover>Stop AAPI Hate Youth Campaign, which interviewed nearly 1,000 young Asian American adults across the country about their experiences with racism during the coronavirus pandemic. Courtesy Beth Yeung, Rebecca Wu, Amanda Young Sept. 17, 2020, 4:30 PM PDT By Claire Wang A 14-year-old student in Dallas was followed home by a group of high school boys who pretended to cough on him and shouted, \"Ching chong! You have Chinese virus!\" A 17-year-old was told over social media that their \"insides are full of 'f---ing bats\" and that they should kill themselves because they are a \"dirty f---ing dog eater.\" An 18-year-old who was grocery shopping was called \"chink\" and told to go back where they came from. These are some of the stories revealed in a new youth-led study showing that one-quarter of Asian American young adults have been the targets of racism over the past year. The discrimination they detail primarily takes the form of verbal harassment, shunning and cyberbullying.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"'You have Chinese virus!': 1 in 4 Asian American youths </cover>","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_94142915#0_185115709","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":4},"model_calls":72,"document_judgments":26,"relevance_3":11,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
